Datasheet: BBO Capstone Project Dataset

This datasheet documents the dataset used for the Black-Box Optimization (BBO) capstone project, following the framework from Mini-lesson 21.1 and reflections from weekly experimental logs. The dataset was created for educational optimisation research and strategy evaluation rather than general machine learning benchmarking.

1. Motivation
Why was this dataset created?

The dataset was created to support a course-based black-box optimisation challenge focused on learning efficient query strategies under evaluation constraints. The primary goal was to maximise unknown black-box objective functions using limited evaluations.

The dataset supports:

Optimization research

Finding input configurations that maximise unknown objective outputs.

Strategy evaluation

Comparing exploration vs exploitation trade-offs.

Testing surrogate modelling approaches.

Educational reflection

Supporting weekly decision analysis and documentation of optimisation behaviour.

The dataset is not intended for general ML model training but rather for optimisation strategy learning and interpretability analysis.

2. Composition
What does the dataset contain?

The dataset contains query–response pairs for multiple black-box functions.

Inputs

Continuous feature vectors bounded in a normalised search space (typically [0,1]^d).

Dimensionality varies per function depending on the optimisation problem.

Outputs

Single scalar objective values.

Higher values correspond to better optimisation performance.

Dataset structure

The dataset is composed of:

Initial sampling data

Weekly optimisation queries

Corresponding function outputs

Example storage structure:

data/
 ├── initial_data/
 ├── week1/
 ├── week2/
 └── weekN/

Each weekly folder contains:

Inputs queried during that week

Observed outputs from black-box evaluation

Size and format

Dataset size grows over time.

Typical observations per function:

Initial dataset: ~10–20 samples per function

Weekly additions: ~1 sample per function per week

Total dataset size depends on project duration

Formats include:

NumPy arrays (.npy)

Text files containing Python-evaluable lists

Gaps and caveats

Spatial gaps

Query locations are non-uniform due to optimisation-driven sampling.

Some regions of the search space remain unexplored.

Temporal gaps

Only a small number of evaluations were allowed per week.

Re-evaluating noisy functions may consume additional query slots.

Noise

Some functions may exhibit stochastic output behaviour, introducing measurement variability.

3. Collection Process
How were queries generated?

Queries were generated using surrogate-based optimisation strategies.

Strategy selection depended on function behaviour and performance trends observed during weekly reflection cycles.

Examples of strategy choices:

Exploration phase (Weeks 1–3)

Random sampling

Uncertainty-driven exploration

Purpose:

Build initial surrogate approximations.

Balanced optimisation phase (Weeks 4–6)

Bayesian optimisation methods

Acquisition functions such as:

Upper Confidence Bound (UCB)

Expected Improvement (EI)

Purpose:

Improve objective values while maintaining model uncertainty coverage.

Refinement phase (Weeks 7–10)

Exploitative optimisation around promising regions.

Local search refinement.

Purpose:

Fine-tune solutions near estimated optima.

Time frame:

Approximately 10–14 weeks of experimentation.

~1 query per function per week under evaluation constraints.

4. Preprocessing and Uses
Transformations applied

Input scaling

Inputs were already normalised to [0,1] search bounds.

Output normalisation

Some pipelines used:

Mean-variance normalisation

Standardisation prior to surrogate training

Predictions were transformed back to original scales when needed.

Intended uses

The dataset supports:

Reproducing BBO optimisation pipelines.

Studying acquisition strategy effectiveness.

Evaluating surrogate modelling decisions.

Supporting academic reflection on optimisation behaviour.

Inappropriate uses

The dataset should not be used for:

❌ General ML model benchmarking
❌ Deployment-level predictive modelling
❌ Inferring true underlying function forms
❌ Training large-scale deep learning systems

Reasons:

Dataset is small and strategy-biased.

Sampling is non-IID and optimisation-driven.

